416108 1077 "selection" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416108 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416089 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416108 1079 #uuid "b8487d6d-ade8-5e6b-aa72-6dcb7d480679" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416106 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416109 1069 "q-126708" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416109 1071 "![](images/e5/e54e2dc5c314ed1dda6ef9c60db5c5df3058ba120afec42bddbabc2622c3ae98.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x+y,\\,-xy⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416109 1072 #uuid "06de88f2-295c-546b-af7c-8eedcb3a386b" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416109 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e1/e1c053e1cc4b4304c1302da2c57278200931b84e4ba687269d0ea89a99b523b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416104 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416110 1069 "q-126753" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416110 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},{y}^{4}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ oriented counterclockwise." 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416110 1072 #uuid "654eb25e-4dd2-5095-910d-a25787a1e455" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416110 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e2/e218f4b6938b166aa6eacab02ce0276a4a863a0cfade8528816a54c2621b51e7.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{4}+{x}^{3},\n$$\n\n we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3})\\,dx+({y}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-(-3{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{81}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{243}{4}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{243\\text{ }\\,\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416105 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416111 1069 "q-126569" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416111 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨y(x-1),\\,x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416111 1072 #uuid "d616e0c6-f6b2-5a83-83cf-32e007120166" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416111 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/b9/b95320928a5e7482f9ba2b8bb1d1499950710545b4c7273b7f70dfe5ab8c84b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y(x-1)$ and $Q(x,y)=x(y-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((y-1)-(x-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416107 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416112 1069 "q-126516" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416112 1071 "![](images/bc/bc0660c37a6658635311a9a973ae3f4802740622a0b35ade22d6050ce648efa6.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416112 1072 #uuid "fdb21538-b802-59ae-83c3-94940f8f1068" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true] [17592186416112 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/77/7724df0035511419dcb5117ba8aa337380325af22ee235d206a954aa12c62fe9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534252 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-05ff21cb-9e33-53f3-b627-a88a79716c30" 17592186416073 "answer-0cf2dceb-7c5d-5d9b-9403-414a88730866" 17592186416074 "answer-10f2c80d-9cac-5756-9929-d92d4f8f3e17" 17592186416075 "answer-168b8872-373b-550c-a253-e7487bbea803" 17592186416076 "answer-1c51a5c2-f91d-5c41-ab32-882df0c70ed8" 17592186416077 "answer-35da7698-acba-5a15-9327-eb06db3b24c8" 17592186416078 "answer-3abf3164-1e19-59b8-a518-c866ee98eb86" 17592186416079 "answer-40901d5c-3eac-5579-8462-7e9d79976125" 17592186416080 "answer-48ad61f7-0826-5aab-ae4e-a6c43c772071" 175921864160